<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A digital lender in Brazil offered personal loans and credit lines through its app. Machine learning models approved, declined and priced applications in seconds, using bureau data, open finance data shared with consent and in-app behaviour. Growth was strong. Complaints were the warning sign: declined customers could not get a useful answer about why, and when the team reviewed a decision it meant piecing together logs from several services.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> Brazil's data protection law, the LGPD, already gives people the right to request review of decisions taken solely through automated processing that affect their interests, including credit profiling, and to receive clear information about the criteria used<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The national data protection authority, ANPD, oversees it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. On top of that, Brazil's AI bill, PL 2338/2023, passed the Senate in December 2024 and was pending in the Chamber of Deputies, with a vote reported as expected after the October 2026 elections<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. It is not law yet, but a lender relying on automated credit decisions could not afford to wait.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every decision reconstructable</strong>: model version, data used and outcome, stored together.</li>
<li><strong>Clear reasons</strong> a customer can understand, without exposing trade secrets.</li>
<li><strong>Human review on request</strong>, handled quickly with full context.</li>
<li><strong>Governed models</strong>: tested before release and monitored after.</li>
<li><strong>Data minimised and documented</strong>, especially open finance data used with consent.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Channels</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>App<em>application and result</em></span><span>Customer service<em>review requests</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Decision</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Credit decision service<em>one entry point</em></span><span>Policy rules<em>hard limits first</em></span><span>Scoring models<em>versioned</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Explain</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Reason codes<em>top factors, plain language</em></span><span>Decision record<em>inputs, versions, outcome</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Review</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Review workbench<em>full context for analysts</em></span><span>Outcome feedback<em>overturns tracked</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Govern</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Model registry<em>tests and approvals</em></span><span>Monitoring<em>drift and stability</em></span><span>Data catalogue<em>purpose and consent</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>One decision service produces the outcome, the reasons and the record in the same step.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Apply</b><span>Application submitted with consented data sources</span></li>
<li><b>Rules</b><span>Policy rules applied first, with their own reasons</span></li>
<li><b>Score</b><span>Versioned model scores; top factors extracted</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Record</b><span>Decision, inputs, versions and reason codes stored together</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Explain</b><span>Customer sees outcome, main reasons and how to ask for review</span></li>
<li><b>Review</b><span>An analyst reviews with full context and can overturn</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>The record is written at decision time, not reconstructed later.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: reconstructing a decision months later</h2>
<p>A review request can arrive weeks or months after the decision. By then models have been retrained and data has changed. The team made the decision record immutable and complete at the moment of decision, and linked each application event to the processing that handled it with distributed tracing. OpenTelemetry instrumentation for Kafka links the receive and process steps of a message in one trace, so analysts can follow an application from arrival to outcome<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/brazil-credit-ai-lgpd-explainability/decision-trace.webp" alt="Jaeger trace view showing a Kafka receive span and a linked process span for the same message, with messaging attributes and references between spans" width="1200" height="345" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Receive and process steps linked in one trace: the same pattern ties each application to the decision made about it. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2022/instrument-kafka-clients/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Paolo Patierno</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>An explanation you generate later is a story. An explanation recorded at decision time is evidence. Build for the second.</p></div>
<p>Reason codes were generated from the model's own attributions and mapped to plain-language statements reviewed by legal and customer teams, so explanations were truthful without revealing model internals. More on evidence and evaluation in <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a> and <a href="/case-studies/korea-ai-basic-act-hiring-platform/">high-impact AI in hiring</a>.</p>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Credit risk</small><ol><li>Policy rules</li><li class="hl">Reason code library</li><li>Model approval</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering and data</small><ol><li>Decision service</li><li class="hl">Decision record and tracing</li><li>Monitoring</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Legal and customer service</small><ol><li>LGPD requirements</li><li>Customer wording</li><li class="hl">Review process</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Risk owned the reasons, engineering the evidence, customer service the review.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Consolidate</b><ul><li>One decision service</li><li>Policy rules separated</li><li>Model registry</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Record</b><ul><li>Immutable decision records</li><li>Tracing</li><li>Data catalogue</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Explain</b><ul><li>Reason codes</li><li>Customer messages</li><li>Review workbench</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Govern</b><ul><li>Release gate</li><li>Drift monitoring</li><li>Overturn analysis</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Consolidating decision logic first made everything else possible.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One decision service</td><td>Scoring spread across services</td><td>One place to record, explain and govern</td></tr>
<tr><td>Record at decision time</td><td>Reconstruct from logs on request</td><td>Models and data change; records must not</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reason codes from model attributions</td><td>Generic decline messages</td><td>Truthful, useful and consistent explanations</td></tr>
<tr><td>Policy rules before models</td><td>Model-only decisions</td><td>Hard limits are easy to explain and audit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prepare for the AI bill now</td><td>Wait for the final text</td><td>The LGPD already requires most of this</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: consolidate decisions into one service before adding explanations; scattered logic cannot be explained. Differently: track overturned decisions from day one. They were the clearest signal of where models or rules needed work. Related: <a href="/insights/structured-outputs-reliable-llm-integration/">structured outputs</a> and <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
<p>If you make automated decisions about customers in Brazil or elsewhere in Latin America, my <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a> explains how I approach it, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
